{
  "input": {
    "rationale": "Sharpening the lease finding's cadence bound. The record said a lease shorter than the typical interval between a leader's contacts with a follower mis-fires, which is directionally right but vague. Since a contact follows either of two independent triggers, the largest gap between contacts is bounded by the smaller of the two intervals, not by their sum or by the heartbeat period; naming that bound makes the finding checkable against a configuration rather than against an impression.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "src_heartbeat_cadence_bound",
        "file": "replication.go",
        "start_line": 398,
        "end_line": 405,
        "kind": "code_observation"
      },
      {
        "id": "src_commit_timeout_bound",
        "file": "replication.go",
        "start_line": 163,
        "end_line": 172,
        "kind": "code_observation"
      }
    ],
    "investigations": [],
    "knowledge": [
      {
        "id": "a8817002f0dc4efeaf230ebbe85d9bff-knowledge-0",
        "ref": null,
        "kind": "fact",
        "statement": "The leader's periodic contact with each follower is driven by two independent timers: the replication heartbeat loop waits a random duration up to one tenth of the heartbeat timeout before each heartbeat, and the replication loop additionally re-replicates on a random duration up to the commit timeout; a response to any of those calls refreshes the follower's contact record. Because either trigger refreshes the record, the largest gap between consecutive contacts for a follower is bounded by the smaller of the two intervals, not by their sum and not by the heartbeat period. A follower counts as contacted for the lease check when the elapsed time since its last response is at most the leader-lease timeout, and the leader steps down when fewer than a quorum are contacted. Configuration validation requires the lease to be at least five milliseconds and at most the heartbeat timeout, and requires the election timeout to be at least the heartbeat timeout; it imposes no lower bound relating the lease to the contact bound. A configuration whose lease is at or below the smaller interval - for example a five-millisecond lease with a one-second heartbeat timeout and the default commit timeout - is therefore accepted, and in it the check will find followers un-contacted much of the time even though responses are arriving on schedule, so the leader logs that it failed to contact a quorum and steps down repeatedly.",
        "conditions": "The mismatch needs a lease below the smaller of the heartbeat cadence and the commit timeout, plus round-trip time; the document frames the condition as being unable to contact a quorum, which a lease below that bound does not represent.",
        "source_ids": [
          "src_heartbeat_cadence_bound",
          "src_commit_timeout_bound"
        ],
        "executions": {},
        "changed_premise": "The earlier statement said a lease shorter than the typical interval between contacts mis-fires, without naming the interval. The operative bound is the smaller of the heartbeat cadence and the commit timeout, because either trigger refreshes the contact record, so the correction replaces an impression with a computable threshold; the mechanism, the validation gap and the bounded consequence are unchanged.",
        "affected_uses": []
      }
    ],
